{"type": "response", "step": 3, "latency_s": 8.031173, "message": {"id": "msg_011CgmHFevo55VcqqUToSfCk", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't roll back prod.orders. The lakehouse blocked the rollback because it would break a data-quality rule, and the table hasn't changed.\n\n**What happened**\n- prod.orders currently has 200 rows, at snapshot 100024 (an append on 2026-11-02).\n- I tried to roll it back to the previous snapshot, 100023 (2026-10-05).\n- The rollback would have dropped 66 rows (33%) from the production table. The limit is 10%, so it was stopped.\n- The system also warned that a rollback is only reversible while the old snapshots are kept. Anyone reading prod.orders would have seen the missing rows until it was restored.\n\nI haven't tried to get around the block. This rule is the kind of safeguard a \"test\" shouldn't override on live production data.\n\n**Safer ways to test rollback**\n1. **Test in development (recommended):** do the same rollback on a table in `db`, such as `db.orders` if it has more than one snapshot. Then check the row count, roll forward again and check that the data comes back. This tests the real process with no risk to prod.\n2. **If it has to be prod:** someone who can approve exceptions to the 33% deletion limit should sign off first. We'd also plan to roll straight back to 100024 afterwards and agree a time window, since readers would see 66 fewer rows in the meantime.\n\nDo you want me to go ahead with option 1?", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1680, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1504, "output_tokens": 479, "output_tokens_detai